ce:abstract-sec.ce:simple-para:
Sagittal stability was reduced more in young (p < 0.001; d ​= ​1.98; 47%) compared to middle-aged (p ​= ​0.002; d ​= ​1.00; 41%) while all other postural stability outcomes were reduced similarly between age groups (p < 0.001–0.002;
η p 2
​= ​0.294–0.695). Torque outcomes remained unchanged for both groups (p ​= ​0.085–0.989;
η p 2
​= ​0.000–0.099).

ce:section.ce:para:
Normality was assessed through assessment of skewness and kurtosis. Skewness and kurtosis values were divided by their standard error and a threshold of 1.96 was used to determine non-normality. Several postural stability variables were non-normally distributed. A log 10 transformation was applied to these data and normality was verified. Baseline comparisons between groups were performed with independent sample t-tests. Two-way (group [young vs. middle-aged] ​× ​time [pre vs. post]) repeated measures analyses of variance were used to examine the effects of the fast-paced walking task on postural stability and torque outcomes. In the case of significant interactions, Bonferroni-corrected pairwise comparisons were examined for the simple effects. Equality of variance was confirmed using Levene's Test for Equality of Variances. Statistical analyses were performed using PASW software version 29.0 (SPSS Inc, Chicago, IL, United States) and an alpha level of p ​≤ ​0.05 was used to determine statistical significance. Effect sizes were reported using partial eta squared (
η p 2
) for analyses of variance and < 0.06, 0.07–0.14, and > 0.14 were used to indicate small, medium, and large effects, respectively, whereas Cohen's d was used for pairwise comparisons with 0.20, 0.50, and 0.80 indicating the same effect size classifications. Data are reported as mean ​± ​SD in the text and box-and-whisker plots using Tukey method are shown in figures. Postural stability data is reported in its original units for interpretation purposes.
